WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Traditionally, testing native mobile applications requires spinning up a full OS simulator.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.000
To verify what a user sees, the testing framework has to launch the app

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.000
and inspect the heavy accessibility hierarchy produced by the native UI toolkit.

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.000
React Native Testing Library follows the same behavioral philosophy

5
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.000
but moves the rendering process into a headless node environment.

6
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.000
This split-screen comparison illustrates the resulting performance gap.

7
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.000
By completely removing the device GPU and the react native bridge,

8
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.000
execution time drops from minutes on a full simulator down to milliseconds in node.

9
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.000
The library achieves high native level fidelity without the native host environment

10
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.000
by strictly enforcing a behavioral testing model.

11
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.000
It gives you a querying vocabulary that forces you to interact with the component

12
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.000
exactly the way a user or a screen reader would.

13
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:55.000
If you are coming from XCU test, your entry point is usually the XCU application instance.

14
00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:00.000
In react native testing library, your entry point is simply the render function.

15
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.000
When render mounts your component in memory, it generates a screen object.

16
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.000
This object holds references to all the query functions scoped to that specific render cycle,

17
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.000
which prevents your tests from holding on to stale variables when a component re-renders.

18
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.000
This diagram shows how we handle production dependencies.

19
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.000
Most real-world components rely on external context,

20
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.000
like a theme provider and authentication session or a query client.

21
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:33.000
To test them, you use a custom wrapper property to supply these nested context layers directly to the render function.

22
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.000
This wrapper is also where you optimize for the test environment.

23
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:43.000
For example, setting retry false on your query client forces react query to fail immediately on bad network requests,

24
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.000
rather than initiating a retry loop that slows down your test runner.

25
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.000
To avoid writing this boilerplate in every file,

26
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:57.000
a standard engineering practice is to export a custom render with provider's helper from a shared utility file,

27
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.000
standardizing the context for your entire test suite.

28
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Isolating the render boundary with mocked providers guarantees that your component logic is tested deterministically,

29
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.000
completely isolated from external network flakiness.

30
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:15.000
When you need to find an element, prioritize its accessibility role over internal implementation tags.

31
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:21.000
This mirrors Apple's long-standing guidance for writing behavior-driven tests that survive code refactors,

32
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:25.000
interact with visible traits of the interface, not the underlying state.

33
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:29.000
This pyramid illustrates the strict query hierarchy you should follow.

34
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:34.000
At the top, in green, you default to get by role and get by text.

35
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:39.000
Moving down, fall back to get by label text if the element lacks visible text,

36
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.000
and at the bottom, in red, is get by test ID.

37
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:48.000
Get by role sits up at top because it reads the exact same accessibility props that are real device uses.

38
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:57.000
A role of button or spin button perfectly mirrors the dot button or dot adjustable UI accessibility traits that voiceover announces to a user on iOS.

39
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.000
The get by test ID query is the absolute last resort.

40
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:09.000
You reserve it exclusively for elements that have no intrinsic semantic meaning, like a custom animated view or a decorative layout container.

41
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.000
By enforcing this hierarchy, your tests pull double duty.

42
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:19.000
They validate the apps' logical behavior, and they implicitly guarantee the interface is accessible to screen readers,

43
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.000
all while remaining immune to structural changes in your DOM.

44
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:28.000
To trigger logic, the library provides two distinct interaction APIs, fire event and user event.

45
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:33.000
This timeline visualizes fire event. It is the older, low-level API.

46
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:41.000
It operates synchronously, bypassing the UI layer to dispatch a synthetic event directly to the components handler function.

47
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.000
A real user interaction is entirely different.

48
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.000
User event simulates the actual sequence of a physical tap.

49
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:57.000
A single press fires a sequential waterfall of pointer events, from touchdown to release, replicating the true life cycle of the gesture.

50
00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:10.000
Because user event interactions share a unified clock and event context, they correctly trigger the complex behaviors that simple synthetic events miss, like debounce text inputs or batched state updates.

51
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.000
The engineering rule here is simple. Always default to user event.

52
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:23.000
It ensures you are testing how the component actually behaves under physical constraints, rather than testing an idealized instantaneous handler invocation.

53
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.000
This brings us to the core challenge of asynchronous UI testing.

54
00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:37.000
Just executes code extremely fast. Often faster than React can process its own render cycle, causing assertions to fire against an incomplete UI.

55
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:47.000
When an interaction triggers a state update outside of a direct event handler, like data arriving from a resolved promise or a delayed timeout, React batches that update.

56
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:57.000
To synchronize the test runner with the component, you use the Act function. Act is the exact equivalent of calling fulfill on an XC test expectation.

57
00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:03.000
It explicitly halts the test until React flushes all pending state updates and effects before continuing.

58
00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:08.000
Manually wrapping code in Act is tedious, so the library provides syntactic sugar.

59
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:15.000
Functions like wait for to pull the interface, or find by to query async elements, handle this boundary automatically.

60
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:23.000
Synchronizing these async boundaries ensures the DOM is entirely settled before the test runner attempts to read it, making assertions predictable.

61
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:39.000
A robust React Native component test follows a strict pipeline, set up the render boundary with mocked providers, query elements by their accessibility roles, trigger reality-based interactions via user events, and await the results using proper async boundaries.

62
00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:46.000
By combining a headless node runtime with voice over mapped queries, this pipeline processes hundreds of assertions in milliseconds.

63
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:55.000
Because these tests ignore implementation details, refactoring a component's internal state shape won't break your suite as long as the visible output remains the same.

64
00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:07.000
This approach covers your logic and layout, but it does have limits. Verifying the actual native bridge, real network calls, and deep navigation stacks requires shifting to end-to-end frameworks like detox.

65
00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:19.000
React Native Testing Library serves as the high-speed structural foundation of your testing pyramid. It proves your component logic and accessibility are flawless long before a heavy mobile simulator is ever launched.

